detailedExplanation.howItWorks:
You define lifecycle rules on a bucket or prefix that transition objects after a number of days and delete them after a retention period. An example S3 lifecycle policy:

```json
{
  "Rules": [{
    "ID": "events-tiering",
    "Filter": { "Prefix": "events/" },
    "Status": "Enabled",
    "Transitions": [
      { "Days": 30, "StorageClass": "STANDARD_IA" },
      { "Days": 90, "StorageClass": "GLACIER" }
    ],
    "Expiration": { "Days": 2555 }
  }]
}
```

Here recent data stays on Standard, moves to Infrequent Access after 30 days, to Glacier after 90, and is deleted after 7 years. Intelligent-Tiering classes can also move data automatically based on observed access, removing the need to predict patterns.
